WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:01:08.944
Welcome back to CPY 701. This is Week Two of sixteen, and
today we examine the great theoretical traditions of
counselling. Last week I told you that what makes the theories
different from one another is not the techniques they use, but
the assumption each one makes about how human beings change.
That claim is the spine of this lecture. We will work through
the psychodynamic, behavioural, cognitive, humanistic and
systemic traditions, and for each one I want you to ask a
single question: what does this theory believe has to happen
for a person to get better? By the end of the session I want
you to be able to answer that question for every major
tradition, to state honestly what the evidence says about
each, and to understand why almost no competent practitioner
today works from one theory alone. Before we start, one
warning. You will be tempted to pick a favourite today and
defend it for the rest of your career. Resist that. You are
not choosing a football team. You are assembling a repertoire.

2
00:01:08.944 --> 00:02:27.399
Here is what you should be able to do by the end of this
session. First, explain why a coherent theory of change is a
professional necessity rather than an academic luxury. Second,
describe the five major traditions, and state the central
mechanism of change that each one proposes. Third, state what
each tradition assumes about human nature, about the origins
of distress, and about the proper role of the counsellor.
Fourth, summarise the current evidence base for each
orientation honestly, without overstating what the research
shows. Fifth, distinguish the four routes to integrative
practice, and identify the risks that come with each. And
sixth, justify a treatment choice for a named client by
reference to evidence, to your own formulation, and to the
client's preference and values. That final objective is the
professional skill this lecture is really about. Notice that
it requires all three sources of information. If your
justification rests on the evidence alone, you are ignoring
the person in front of you. If it rests on your formulation
alone, you are ignoring the science. And if it rests on the
client's preference alone, you are not offering professional
expertise at all.

3
00:02:27.399 --> 00:03:09.167
Let us begin with a question that students sometimes find
impertinent but which I want you to take seriously. Why do we
need theory at all? If the evidence tells us which treatments
work, and if kindness and listening matter more than anything
else, could we not simply do what works and leave the
theorising to academics? The answer is no, and the reason is
not that academics need employment. It is that in a room with
a distressed person, you must decide, moment by moment, what
to attend to, what to say, what to ignore and what to do next.
Theory is what tells you how to decide.

4
00:03:09.167 --> 00:04:44.258
Here is the working definition for today. A theory of
counselling is an organised set of assumptions about human
functioning, about how distress and disorder arise, and about
the mechanisms by which change occurs. From those assumptions,
a theory generates a rationale for what the counsellor should
attend to, what should be done, and in what order. Let me draw
your attention to the logical order in that sentence, because
it is the reverse of the order in which students usually meet
this material. Assumptions about human nature come first. The
mechanism of change follows from those assumptions. And
technique comes last, as the practical consequence of the
first two. Now consider what this means when two practitioners
use the same technique. A psychodynamic counsellor and a
cognitive behavioural therapist may both invite a client to
describe an early memory of a parent. The technique looks
identical. But the psychodynamic practitioner is attending to
the internalised relationship and expecting insight to do the
work, while the cognitive practitioner is attending to the
belief formed in that memory and expecting the revision of
that belief to do the work. Same action, different theory,
different destination. This is why I said on the first slide
that technique is the wrong place to look for what separates
the traditions. The separation lives above the technique, in
the assumptions.

5
00:04:44.258 --> 00:07:01.639
A theory earns its place by doing work, not by being elegant
or by being famous. Here are the four jobs I need a theory to
do. First, it organises the chaos. A distressed client arrives
with an undifferentiated mass of material: a recent argument,
a difficult childhood, a physical symptom, a financial worry,
a suspicion about a colleague, a fear about the future. All of
it is real, and all of it is vivid. Theory is what tells you
what to attend to and what to let pass for now. And notice why
this matters practically. Without a theory you will attend to
whatever is most vivid in the room, and the most vivid thing
is very often not the most useful thing. The client who cries
hardest about their employer may be presenting the surface of
a problem that lives in their marriage. Second, theory makes
your reasoning examinable. If you can name what you are doing
and why, a supervisor can challenge it, and you can learn. If
your reasoning is a private intuition that you cannot
articulate, it can neither be taught nor corrected nor
defended. This is the difference between a professional and a
well-meaning person with good instincts. Third, a theory of
change predicts what should happen next. If the mechanism you
have proposed is right, then this intervention should shift
that outcome, and the shift should be measurable. Expectations
can be tested against evidence. Hunches cannot be tested at
all, which is precisely why they feel so reliable. Fourth, and
this is the job students find hardest to accept: theory
protects your client from your personal preferences. Every one
of you has a life story that makes certain explanations
emotionally satisfying. If your own family dismissed your
feelings, you may find yourself drawn strongly to
interpretations about suppressed emotion, and you will find
them everywhere. Theory forces you to justify why your
favoured explanation fits this client, rather than only
fitting you. That is not a small protection. It is the
difference between practice and projection.

6
00:07:01.639 --> 00:07:54.380
We turn now to the traditions themselves. I want to be clear
about how I am organising this, because there is more than one
legitimate way to divide the field. I am going to group the
theories by the mechanism of change they propose, because that
is the question that matters most in practice. What has to
happen for this person to get better? For the psychodynamic
traditions, the answer involves making the unconscious
conscious. For the behavioural traditions, it involves new
learning. For the cognitive traditions, revised beliefs. For
the humanistic traditions, the removal of conditions of worth
so that the person's own growth tendency can operate. And for
the systemic traditions, a change in the patterns that
surround the individual. Five families, five different answers
to the same question.

7
00:07:54.380 --> 00:10:10.340
The psychodynamic tradition begins with Freud and has been
transformed almost beyond recognition since his time. Its core
assumption is that much of mental life occurs outside
awareness. Early relationships are internalised as working
models, or internal objects, and those models then shape later
relationships, including, importantly, the relationship the
client forms with you. The mechanism of change follows from
that assumption. The client gains insight into a repeating
pattern, and simultaneously experiences a relationship in
which the old pattern does not repeat. Both halves are
necessary. Insight without a corrective experience tends to
produce a client who can explain their problem beautifully and
still has it. There are four concepts you must know by name.
Transference is the client's displacement onto you of feelings
and expectations that originally belonged to an earlier
relationship. Countertransference is the mirror image: your
own emotional response to the client, which is sometimes a
source of information about what the client evokes in others.
Defence mechanisms are the processes by which the mind keeps
unacceptable material out of awareness, and they are not
pathology. They are the mind protecting itself. And the
repetition compulsion names the striking human tendency to
reproduce a painful pattern rather than to remember and
resolve it, which is why a client may recreate with you
exactly the relational dynamic that hurt them. On the
evidence, I want to correct a prejudice you may have absorbed.
The claim that psychodynamic therapy has no evidence is simply
false. Shedler's review in American Psychologist in 2010
summarised effect sizes comparable to those of other
established treatments, with gains that appeared to increase
after therapy ended. Modern brief psychodynamic treatments
have decent support, particularly for depression and panic.
The tradition has real weaknesses, including a long history of
unfalsifiable claims and of therapies that ran for years
without outcome measurement, but the evidence is not one of
them.

8
00:10:10.340 --> 00:12:11.307
The behavioural and cognitive traditions are often spoken of
together, as CBT, and in practice they are usually delivered
together. But their mechanisms of change are different, and
you should be able to separate them. The behavioural tradition
holds that distress is learned, and therefore can be
unlearned. Its mechanism is new learning: extinction,
exposure, and the modification of reinforcement contingencies.
Its focus is on observable behaviour and what follows it. Its
core methods include exposure, behavioural activation and
skills training. Its evidence base is the strongest of any
tradition, particularly for the anxiety disorders and
obsessive compulsive disorder, where exposure-based work is
the treatment of first choice. The cognitive tradition holds
that distress is driven less by events than by how the person
appraises those events. Its mechanism is the identification
and testing of biased beliefs. Its focus is on automatic
thoughts, the intermediate rules a person holds about how life
must work, and the deeper core beliefs about the self. Its
methods include thought records, behavioural experiments and
Socratic questioning. Its evidence base is very strong,
especially for depression. Now here is why the distinction
matters in practice. Exposure works even when the client's
beliefs are unchanged at the start, because the learning
happens through experience rather than through argument. And
cognitive restructuring does nothing if the client merely
understands it intellectually, because the belief has not
actually been tested against experience. Notice that both
traditions converge on the same practical principle: change
requires the client to encounter disconfirming experience.
Telling a person that their fear is irrational changes very
little. Arranging for them to discover that it is unfounded
changes a great deal. If you take one practical lesson from
this slide, take that one.

9
00:12:11.307 --> 00:14:11.880
The humanistic tradition, and specifically the person centred
therapy that Carl Rogers developed, rests on a claim about
human nature that is quite different from anything we have met
so far. Rogers held that human beings possess an actualising
tendency, an innate drive towards growth, maturity and the
fulfilment of potential. If that is true, then the
counsellor's job is not to construct something in the client.
It is to remove what is in the way. Distress, on this account,
arises when conditions of worth interfere with the actualising
tendency. A child who is only loved when they perform, or only
accepted when they suppress anger, learns that their worth is
conditional. They then internalise that condition and apply it
to themselves, and the parts of their experience that do not
meet it are denied. The task of therapy is to provide the
relational conditions under which the actualising tendency can
resume, and Rogers named three: empathy, which is accurate
understanding of the client's frame of reference;
unconditional positive regard, which is acceptance of the
client without conditions attached; and congruence, which is
the counsellor's authenticity, the refusal to hide behind a
professional mask. Rogers went further than most people
realise. In his 1957 paper he claimed these conditions were
not merely helpful but necessary and sufficient. Necessary,
meaning that without them no therapy would work. Sufficient,
meaning that with them, nothing else was required. That second
claim is the one that has been most contested, and you should
know both the claim and the criticism. What has survived, and
what has been absorbed into almost every other approach, is
the finding that the therapeutic relationship is a powerful
predictor of outcome. That idea began here, and it is now one
of the most replicated findings in the field.

10
00:14:11.880 --> 00:16:27.840
The systemic tradition changes the unit of analysis, and that
is its whole contribution. Its core assumption is that the
problem lives between people, not only inside one person. An
individual's symptoms are understood as serving a function
within a relational system, and the person who is presented as
the patient, whom early systemic writers called the identified
patient, is rarely the whole story. Consider a child who
develops severe behavioural problems. A linear account says
the child has a disorder and the task is to treat the child. A
systemic account asks a different question. What happens in
this family when the child's behaviour escalates? Does it draw
a distant parent back into the home? Does it unite two parents
who otherwise argue? If either is true, then the behaviour is
doing relational work, and treating the child alone may
relieve the system's pressure by removing its function, which
the system may then resist. The second core idea is circular
rather than linear causality. Behaviour is understood as
mutual influence rather than one-directional cause and effect.
Not she nags because he withdraws, but both, in a loop that
each person's behaviour sustains. This does not mean nobody is
responsible for anything, and I want to be careful here,
because circular causality has sometimes been used to excuse
harm. It means that blame is a poor guide to intervention, and
that intervening anywhere in the loop may change the whole.
The concepts you should know by name are homeostasis, the
system's tendency to resist change and return to its familiar
state; feedback loops; boundaries, which may be too rigid or
too diffuse; triangulation, in which two people manage their
tension by involving a third; enmeshment; and the identified
patient. On the evidence, family based treatment has strong
support in several conditions, most notably eating disorders
in adolescents, where family based treatment is a first line
approach, and relapse prevention in psychosis, where family
intervention measurably reduces relapse rates. Week Five,
Family Counseling, will take this tradition much further.

11
00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:59.865
Part Three puts the traditions side by side, because the
differences between them are clearest when you examine their
assumptions rather than their techniques. This is the
comparison I want you to carry in your head, and I am going to
give it to you as a single table that you can reconstruct from
memory. For each tradition, hold three things: what it
believes is the source of the problem, what it believes the
client must experience for change to occur, and who it
believes is the principal agent of that change.

12
00:16:59.865 --> 00:19:35.141
This table is the heart of the lecture, and I want you to read
it down the columns rather than across the rows, because the
contrasts are the point. Take the third column, what change
requires. The psychodynamic traditions say change requires
insight into a repeating pattern, and it must happen within a
new relationship. The behavioural traditions say change
requires new learning through disconfirming experience. The
cognitive traditions say change requires testing beliefs
against evidence and experience. The humanistic traditions say
change requires the relational conditions of empathy,
unconditional positive regard and congruence. And the systemic
traditions say change requires altering the pattern, often
with the whole system in the room. Now look at the fourth
column, the agent of change, because this is where the
traditions differ most sharply and where they are most
frequently blurred. For the psychodynamic and humanistic
traditions, the principal agent is the client's own process,
enabled or released by the counsellor. For the cognitive
tradition, the agent is a collaborative examination that the
counsellor actively structures. For the behavioural tradition,
the agent is structured practice, much of which happens
between sessions rather than inside them. And for the systemic
tradition, the agent is the relational system itself, which is
why systemic practitioners will happily work with people who
are not the referred client. One observation I want to leave
with you. Ranking these traditions from most directive to
least directive gives you: behavioural, then cognitive, then
systemic, then psychodynamic, then humanistic. That spectrum
is useful, because client preference tends to follow it. Some
clients want structure and homework. Others find homework
patronising and want to be understood. Both are legitimate,
and matching the approach to the person is part of the skill.
And one further observation about the systemic column. It is
the only tradition in this table that does not locate the
problem inside a single mind, and that difference has
practical consequences you will meet immediately on placement.
A systemic practitioner may decline to treat the referred
client alone, not out of inflexibility, but because the theory
holds that changing an individual while the surrounding
pattern is untouched will often merely move the problem rather
than resolve it.

13
00:19:35.141 --> 00:21:31.563
Before we turn to integration, I need to give you the evidence
about what actually accounts for change, because it reframes
everything else. When researchers decompose the variance in
therapy outcomes, the picture looks roughly like this. The
largest share is accounted for by the client and their
context: their resources, the severity of the problem, their
social support, and the life events that occur during
treatment. You cannot control most of this, and you should not
pretend to. A substantial further share is accounted for by
the alliance, meaning agreement on goals, agreement on tasks,
and the relational bond. This holds across every orientation,
which tells you it is not a feature of any one theory. Another
portion is accounted for by allegiance and expectancy: the
practitioner's belief in what they are doing, and the client's
hope that it will help. And technique, the specific methods
that distinguish the orientations, accounts for a real and
measurable difference, but a smaller one than the factors
already named. Now, misread this and you will draw a dangerous
conclusion. Technique accounts for a smaller share, therefore
technique does not matter. That is wrong, and the evidence
does not support it. The specific methods are what make the
difference between treatments that work for a condition and
treatments that do not. Exposure works for obsessive
compulsive disorder and relaxation training does not. That
difference is technique, and it is decisive. What the common
factors evidence tells us is something subtler and more
demanding: that a well-delivered treatment delivering the
wrong method is ineffective, and so is a poorly delivered
treatment delivering the right method. You need both, and the
pressure on you is therefore to be technically competent and
relationally skilled. Any training programme that treats those
as alternatives has misunderstood its job.

14
00:21:31.563 --> 00:22:06.973
Part Four addresses the question you will actually face in
practice. Which of these traditions should you use? The honest
answer, supported by how practitioners behave rather than by
what they claim, is that most experienced clinicians use more
than one. Surveys consistently find that the largest single
group of practitioners identifies as integrative or eclectic
rather than as adherents of a single orientation. So the
professional question is not which theory is true, but how to
combine theories responsibly.

15
00:22:06.973 --> 00:24:40.758
There are four routes to integration, and I want to name them
precisely, because not all integration is equal. Three of
these four are defensible. One is usually not. The first is
technical eclecticism. You select techniques from different
traditions according to what the evidence says works for this
particular problem. This is defensible, provided that your
selection is genuinely driven by the evidence rather than by
what you happen to find comfortable. The danger is a
practitioner who claims to be eclectic and is in fact doing
whatever they like, with the evidence as a post-hoc
justification. The second route is theoretical integration, in
which you blend two or more theories into a single coherent
model. Cognitive analytic therapy, which brings together
psychodynamic and cognitive ideas, is the best known example,
as is schema therapy. This route is demanding, but
intellectually serious. It requires you to reconcile the
assumptions, not merely the techniques, and that is hard work.
The third route is assimilative integration, and it is the
most common in real practice. You work from a firm primary
orientation, but you absorb techniques from elsewhere without
abandoning the underlying theory. So a psychodynamic
practitioner might use a behavioural activation plan because
the client's depression demands it, while still understanding
the case psychodynamically. The key is that the primary theory
remains the source of coherence. The fourth route has no
defenders but many practitioners, and I want you to be able to
recognise it in yourself. It is syncretism: mixing approaches
without any rationale, because it feels helpful in the moment.
This is not integration. It is drift. And when integration is
principled, the practitioner can explain why they chose a
technique. When it is drift, the practitioner cannot, and the
client carries the cost of that incoherence. The test is
simple and you should apply it to yourself throughout your
training: can I explain, in advance, why I am doing this with
this client? I want to add one caution about assimilative
integration, because it is the route most of you will take and
it has a specific failure mode. The primary orientation has to
remain the source of your coherence. If you begin borrowing
techniques whose assumptions contradict your primary theory,
and you do not notice, you are no longer practising
assimilative integration. You are practising syncretism with a
respectable name attached to it.

16
00:24:40.758 --> 00:27:24.619
Now the evidence, stated as honestly as I can state it. First,
for most common conditions, several orientations work.
Depression, for example, responds to cognitive therapy,
behavioural activation, brief psychodynamic therapy and person
centred work. When the effects are compared head to head, the
differences are usually small, and the finding that all of the
major therapies have won is closer to the truth than the
marketing of any single school. This is the famous dodo
verdict, named after the bird in Alice in Wonderland that
declared everybody has won and all must have prizes. Second,
and this is the important qualification, for some conditions
the specific method is decisive. Exposure with response
prevention for obsessive compulsive disorder is the clearest
example. Behavioural therapy for specific phobias is another.
In these cases, the wrong method does not work however well it
is delivered, and the right method works even in fairly
ordinary hands. So the dodo verdict is true for some
conditions and false for others, and you need to know which is
which. Third, more is not better. Most clients who are going
to improve do so early. Routinely extending treatment beyond
eight to ten sessions produces diminishing returns for most
presentations. This is uncomfortable for practitioners,
because there is always more to explore, and exploring feels
like diligence. It is not diligence if it displaces the
results the client came for. Fourth, and I want this stated
plainly because it affects how you interpret everything else:
the evidence base is not culturally representative. The trials
behind these claims were overwhelmingly conducted on Western
samples, mostly in North America and Europe. Extrapolating
those findings to a Nigerian client in Ibadan is an
assumption, not a finding. I am not saying the treatments do
not work here, and a great deal of clinical experience
suggests many of them do. I am saying that the confident
version of these claims is not supported by the evidence, and
part of your professional integrity is stating the uncertainty
rather than borrowing the confidence. Let me make that
practical. Knowing which situation you are in means asking,
before you choose, whether this presentation is one where the
major approaches converge or one where the method is decisive.
Depression and adjustment difficulties are usually the former.
Obsessive compulsive disorder, phobias and panic are usually
the latter. Getting that judgment wrong is one of the most
consequential errors a beginning practitioner can make,
because in the second group, delivering the wrong method
competently still fails.

17
00:27:24.619 --> 00:30:54.094
Let us apply this to a case. Chidi is thirty four, a secondary
school teacher, referred after four months of low mood,
disturbed sleep, and a marked withdrawal from colleagues he
used to enjoy. He describes himself as not good enough, and he
says he has felt this way for as long as he can remember,
though it worsened after he was passed over for promotion. He
grew up with a father who was warm when Chidi excelled and
silent when he did not. And his wife reports that he
apologises constantly, including for things that are plainly
not his fault. Now I want you to notice something about this
vignette before we reach the questions. Every fact I have
given you is doing double duty. The apology is a behavioural
observation, a cognitive belief made audible, a relational
pattern, and a systemic event. This is precisely why the
traditions diverge in what they attend to. Let us take them in
order. Psychodynamic: what pattern is repeating, and where
will it appear in your consulting room? The pattern is worth
conditional on performance, learned from a father whose warmth
was contingent. And you should expect it to appear with you.
Chidi will very likely arrive apologetic, eager to be a good
client, anxious about your approval and sensitive to any sign
of disappointment. That is transference, and it is also your
best evidence. Cognitive: what core belief is at work, and
what keeps it intact? The core belief is something close to I
am only acceptable when I perform, with the promotion decision
functioning as confirmation. What keeps it intact is that he
selects, remembers and attends to evidence that fits, and he
apologises pre-emptively, which prevents anyone from actually
disconfirming him. Behavioural: what has he stopped doing, and
how is that maintaining the low mood? He has withdrawn from
colleagues, which removes the social contact and reinforcement
that would lift his mood. This is a textbook downward spiral,
and it is also the fastest thing you can change. Humanistic:
what condition of worth did he internalise, and what would he
have to experience from you? He internalised the condition
that warmth is earned. To loosen it he would need to
experience, not merely be told, that your regard for him is
not contingent on performance. Person centred practitioners
call that unconditional positive regard, and it is harder to
provide than it sounds with a client who apologises
constantly. Systemic: how does his withdrawal affect his
marriage, and what function might his self blame serve? His
self blame and apology may be quietly managing conflict, by
pre-empting any accusation before it is made. If so, then
reducing his self blame may increase tension at home in the
short term, and you should prepare for that rather than be
surprised by it. Finally, the real question. Which formulation
would you test first, and what evidence would change your
mind? There is no single right answer, and I am not going to
pretend there is. The answer depends on severity, on risk, on
what he wants, and on what is available. But whatever you
choose, you must be able to state what evidence would lead you
to abandon it. That is what makes it a formulation rather than
an assumption.

18
00:30:54.094 --> 00:32:34.505
Let me consolidate. Five takeaways. One. A theory of
counselling is a set of assumptions about human functioning,
from which a mechanism of change follows, and from which
technique follows last. It is the assumptions, not the
techniques, that separate the traditions. Two. Five families,
five mechanisms of change. Insight into repeating patterns
within a new relationship. New learning through disconfirming
experience. The revision of biased beliefs. The release of an
actualising tendency that conditions of worth had blocked. And
the alteration of patterns of interaction that sustain the
symptom. Three. The common factors evidence tells you to
become technically competent and relationally skilled. It does
not license you to be either one instead of the other, and any
training programme that treats them as alternatives has
misunderstood its job. Four. For most common conditions,
several orientations perform comparably. For a smaller number
of conditions, the specific method is decisive, and the
clearest example is exposure with response prevention for
obsessive compulsive disorder. You must know which situation
you are in before you choose. Five. Integration is legitimate
when you can explain in advance why you chose a method for
this client. When you cannot explain it in advance, it is not
integration. It is drift. And the test of drift is that the
practitioner reaches for the rationale afterwards, whereas the
principled integrator has it beforehand.

19
00:32:34.505 --> 00:33:55.691
Your reading for the coming week. Read Chapter Seven of Gelso,
Nutt Williams and Fretz, which surveys the theoretical
approaches, alongside Chapter Two of the Woolfe Handbook,
whose British framing usefully differs from the American text
on several points you should notice. Read Shedler's 2010 paper
on the efficacy of psychodynamic psychotherapy. Read it
carefully, and notice where Shedler concedes limitations. A
source that concedes nothing is a source you should trust
less, not more. Read Rogers' 1957 paper in full. As I said, it
is short, and it is the founding statement of the most
influential idea in our field. Read Standard Two of the APA
Ethics Code, on competence, and consider what it demands of a
newly qualified practitioner who has been trained mainly in
one orientation. Finally, your preparation for Week Three. In
your reflective journal, write four to six hundred words on
the following. Having met all five traditions, which one are
you most drawn to, and what in your own history might explain
that attraction? I want you to be specific and honest. Of all
the exercises this semester, that is the one that most repays
candour, because the answer you write down privately, and do
not have to defend, is the one that will tell you where your
blind spots are likely to be.

20
00:33:55.691 --> 00:35:00.000
That brings us to the end of Week Two. Thank you for staying
with a lecture that has covered a great deal of conceptual
ground. Next week we leave theory for practice and examine
counselling techniques and interventions: attending,
listening, questioning, reflecting, and above all the
discipline of staying with the client instead of reaching for
the impressive formulation. You will notice that the skills we
study next week belong to no single tradition. That is exactly
the point, and it is the practical bridge between what we did
today and everything that follows. Before then, three things.
Read the four items indicated, and give Shedler and Rogers
real attention rather than skimming them. Submit your
reflective journal entry by Friday at twenty three fifty nine.
And bring your honest answer to the question of which
tradition attracts you. In seminar we will discuss the pattern
across the group, not the individuals in it, and I think you
will find the pattern instructive. I will see you next week.
